      "id": 1,
      "text": "Hudson was laid out by Horatio Petit on August 13, 1836 in Illinois.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hudson%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Illinois Land Association founded Hudson as a colony, drawing lots so settlers shared land and profits.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20,
        14,
        26,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hudson%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Hudson’s original layout had no central square, and its “in lots” were widely spaced.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hudson%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Illinois Central Railroad arrived in 1854 just west of Hudson, securing the town’s growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hudson%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Hudson was named after Hudson, New York, reflecting the origins of several early settlers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
